_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 18
---
order: 18
---
short_label: VI.18
---
statement: To describe a rectilinear figure similar and similarly situated to a given rectilinear figure on a given straight line.
---
red_highlight: yes
---
proof:

Let *AB* be the given straight line and *CE* the given rectilinear figure.

It is required to describe on the straight line *AB* a rectilinear figure similar and similarly situated to the rectilinear figure *CE.*

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="350" height="270" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="VI.18 diagram" src="propVI18.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "VI.18",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,240;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;1000,240;0;0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,220,0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,310,0",
            "C;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,30,0",
            "D;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,160,0",
            "E;point;free;130,30",
            "F;point;free;60,130",
            "CDEF;polygon;quadrilateral;C,D,E,F;0;0;black;random",
            "DF;line;connect;D,F",
            "G;point;similar;A,B,C,D,F",
            "ABG;polygon;triangle;A,B,G;0;0;black;random",
            "H;point;similar;B,G,D,F,E",
            "BGH;polygon;triangle;B,G,H;0;0;black;random"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Join *DF.*  Construct the angle *GAB* equal to the angle at *C,* and the angle *ABG* equal to the angle *CDF,* on the straight line *AB* at the points *A* and *B* on it. [!just I.23]

Therefore the remaining angle *CFD* equals the angle *AGB.* Therefore the triangle *FCD* is equiangular with the triangle *GAB.* [!just I.32]

Therefore, proportionally, *FD* is to *GB* as *FC* is to *GA,* and as *CD* is to *AB.* [!just VI.4; V.16]

Again, construct the angle *BGH* equal to the angle *DFE,* and the angle *GBH* equal to the angle *FDE,* on the straight line *BG* and at the points *B* and *G* on it. [!just I.23]

Therefore the remaining angle at *E* equals the remaining angle at *H.*  Therefore the triangle *FDE* is equiangular with the triangle *GBH.*  Therefore, proportionally, *FD* is to *GB* as *FE* is to *GH,* and as *ED* is to *HB.* [!just I.32; VI.4; V.16]

But it was also proved that *FD* is to *GB* as *FC* is to *GA,* and as *CD* is to *AB.* Therefore  *FC* is to *AG* as *CD* is to *AB,* and as *FE* is to *GH,* and further as *ED* is to *HB.* [!just V.11]

And, since the angle *CFD* equals the angle *AGB,* and the angle *DFE* equals the angle *BGH,* therefore the whole angle *CFE* equals the whole angle *AGH.*

For the same reason the angle *CDE* also equals the angle *ABH.*

And the angle at *C* also equals the angle at *A,* and the angle at *E* equals the angle at *H.*

Therefore *AH* is equiangular with *CE,* and they have the sides about their equal angles proportional.  Therefore the rectilinear figure *AH* is similar to the rectilinear figure *CE.* [!just VI.Def.1]

Therefore the rectilinear figure *AH* has been described similar and similarly situated to the given rectilinear figure *CE* on the given straight line *AB.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
---
body:

It is evident from the diagram, if not from the text, that *AB* should correspond to *CD.*

Although the figure has only four sides, it is clear that the method applies to figures with more than four sides.

This proposition is used in the proofs of propositions @VI.22, @VI.25, and @VI.28, and the corollary is used in @XII.17.
